71K ranks in the bottom quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers, improving at 35%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $2K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $784 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 38\u00d7 revenue per employee (elevated relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Dutch Bros Inc. is a mid-cap consumer cyclical business with a $5.9B market cap on $1.6B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +30% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder. Operating margins are expanding by +8.1pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.","Returns on capital run at 9% against a 12% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $54M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 3 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 3.70\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage runs at 4.1\u00d7.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 3.0% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 45% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.4458390818894109},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (49.1% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat"